Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: a rounding tried again over a narrow face's surface unbounded
(ChamferOverNarrowFace, patches 0531 and 0940) walked its guides past the
spine's ends and approximated them in full, though its cut by the faces
beyond (ChamferCutByFacesBeyond) was refused for a reason its analytic
pieces showed before the walk (mitcad#1824, mitcad#2065).

The case: a ring wall 3 thick with five windows, each window's floor and
ceiling rounded with 1.5 on both edges (geometry.fillet_consumed_strip,
the case of patch 0950). Each rounding is tried over its floor's or
ceiling's plane unbounded: its piece within the spine is the analytic
torus (PerformSetOfKPart), whose contact on the floor reaches the
pillars' half-round ends and so leaves the floor; the walk
(PerformSetOfKGen) then runs past the spine's ends over the unbounded
plane until the guide's normal plane grazes the wall, where the sections
slide along the wall while the guide barely moves. The approximation of
those pieces (Approx_SweepApproximation) cut each into up to its 5000
segments and still missed the tolerance (about 90 % of the fillet's time
in stack samples). The cut then refused the try at the analytic piece
(its step 1 takes a crossing of B's boundary only on a walked piece), the
stripe was computed again as before (PerformSetOfSurf), and all ten tries
were thrown away: 21 to 27 s of the fillet's time.

- ChFi3d_NarrowCutRefusedByAnalyticPieces: after the analytic pieces of
  the try, before the walk, a piece within the spine that is not walked
  (ChFi3d_NarrowPieceWalked, the test step 1 uses, now shared) and whose
  contact on B, at step 1's samples, changes between inside and outside B
  refuses the try: ChamferCutByFacesBeyond's step 1 returns false at such
  a piece whatever the pieces before it gave, the walk adds pieces past or
  between the analytic ones and leaves those as they are, and every
  earlier exit of the cut is a refusal too. Only pieces whose line lies on
  the unbounded face with a p-curve are read; any other piece leaves the
  decision to the cut as before.
- Such a try walks its guides within the spine as before and leaves out
  only the guides past the spine's ends (ChFi3d_NarrowNotPast, read by
  PerformSetOfKGen), whose pieces the refusal throws away; such a guide
  leaves the builder done, as it did walked or failed (its failure was
  caught there). ChamferOverNarrowFace then returns false, as the cut did.
  A failure that is not a computation failure (mitcad#1986) is passed on
  as before, with the flag cleared.

The guides within the spine stay walked because what they leave goes on
to the stripe computed again after the refusal. On a reported design two
bosses on a plate were rounded where each try's walk from the spine's
start failed at once (a walking failure): the builder's done flag the
failed walk left false is not set again by the stripe computed again
from analytic pieces alone, so the stripe was reported failed and the
rounding made otherwise, the plate's end face split into five pieces that
later features name. A first version of this patch (1696 of 18.11.2026)
skipped the whole walk, left done true and the end face whole, and those
features failed.

The result is the same: the fillet of the consumed-strip case written as
BRep is the same byte for byte, in about 3 s instead of 21 to 34 s; the
reported design's faces and edges are named the same as without the
patch (mitcad-cli info --names); and OCCT's blend and chamfer DRAW cases
(754) give the same status and results.

Upstream: new (the narrow-face tries are Mitcad's own).

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 1696.
